        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a natural infinite family of equational theorem-proving tasks over integer or finite-field polynomial identities {T_p}, indexed by primes p, such that when a fixed completion or rewrite-based prover is run on T_p, the normalized proof-search trajectory exhibits a detectable Frobenius-periodic signature in its critical-pair graph statistics (for example, cycle counts or spectral radius), and this signature predicts whether the prover finds a short proof in polylogarithmic versus polynomial time in p. Test: Construct explicit families T_p from polynomial identities with reduction behavior mod p; run Knuth-Bendix/completion or Gr\u00f6bner-style provers across many primes; measure graph invariants of the induced search dynamics; confirm if a periodic/arithmetic predictor significantly separates easy and hard primes, and refute if no such statistically stable separation appears. Impact: This would reveal that automated proof difficulty can undergo number-theoretic phase changes controlled by arithmetic dynamics, enabling prime-aware theorem-generation benchmarks and a new bridge between finite-field arithmetic, symbolic computation, and proof complexity.",
