        "id": "fd_0344",
        "title": "p-adic Stability Radius Predicts Gradient Descent Escape from ReLU Saddles",
        "description": "Conjecture: For every fixed-depth ReLU network with rational parameters and rational dataset, each isolated non-global critical point x of the empirical loss has an associated finite set of primes S_x such that, for all sufficiently large primes p not in S_x, the p-adic Newton polygon of the local piecewise-polynomial Hessian germ at x determines whether small rational perturbations admit a descent path escaping x over Q. Precisely, nontrivial lower slopes in the p-adic Hessian spectrum occur if and only if there exists a rational perturbation sequence x_n -> x with strictly decreasing loss. Test: Enumerate critical points for small rational ReLU models, compute local polynomial regions and Hessians, then compare escape/non-escape behavior under rational perturbations against the predicted p-adic slope criterion across many primes. A single stable family of counterexamples refutes it. Impact: This would create a new arithmetic lens on optimization landscapes, linking non-Archimedean geometry to trainability and offering prime-sensitive certificates for spurious minima versus escapable saddles.",
