        "id": "fd_0344",
        "title": "p-adic Shadow Simplicity of Gradient Dynamics",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let f \\in \\mathbb{Z}[x_1,\\dots,x_n] be a polynomial of total degree d with bounded coefficients, and consider gradient descent over \\mathbb{Q}_p on f with fixed step size \\eta \\in p\\mathbb{Z}_p chosen so the update map T(x)=x-\\eta\\nabla f(x) preserves \\mathbb{Z}_p^n. Then for generic such f, the reduction of T modulo p^k has eventually bounded orbit-complexity: there exists C=C(n,d) such that for all k, the functional graph of T on (\\mathbb{Z}/p^k\\mathbb{Z})^n has at most C attractor cycles, and every cycle lifts from a critical point of f mod p. Test: Sample polynomial families and primes p, compute the mod p^k functional graphs for increasing k, and check whether the number of attractor cycles stabilizes uniformly and whether each cycle corresponds to a Hensel lift of a mod p critical point; a single generic counterfamily with unbounded new cycles refutes it. Impact: This would create a new arithmetic theory of optimization dynamics, linking non-Archimedean geometry, dynamical systems, and learning theory; it could enable certified coarse descriptions of loss landscapes via finite-ring reductions.",
