        "description": "Conjecture: There exists an explicit family of polynomial optimization problems f_n : A_n^d -> A_n over Z with bounded degree and dimension growing with n such that, for infinitely many primes p, the reduction mod p of one-step gradient descent x -> x - eta * grad(f_n)(x) on F_p^d exhibits a sharp dichotomy controlled by the factorization type of a discriminant polynomial Delta_n(p): when Delta_n is a quadratic residue mod p, a positive-density set of initial conditions flows into O(1) attracting cycles, while when Delta_n is a nonresidue mod p, the map has no attracting cycles of length <= C and typical orbit length is Omega(p^alpha) for some fixed alpha > 0. Test: Construct an explicit family f_n and eta, compute the finite-field dynamical systems for many primes p, and check whether orbit statistics correlate with the residue class of Delta_n(p) with density bounded away from 0; refute by finding no such family or no statistically significant residue-controlled phase split. Impact: This would create a new bridge between optimization dynamics, arithmetic geometry, and finite dynamical systems, suggesting that training dynamics can carry hidden number-theoretic phase information and enabling arithmetic stress-tests for optimization algorithms.",
