        "id": "fd_0368",
        "title": "Proof-Complexity Shadow of Tropical Degeneration",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let {F_t} be an explicit one-parameter family of polynomial systems over \u211a whose Newton polytopes are fixed and whose coefficients have t-adic valuations inducing a tropical limit T. Then, for a natural propositional encoding SAT(F_t,m) of existence of solutions modulo p^m, the minimum resolution width and clause space of SAT(F_t,m) are asymptotically determined (up to constant factors on an infinite subsequence of m) by a combinatorial obstruction parameter of T, such as the minimum codimension of a balanced cell cover separating all tropical lifts. Test: Construct explicit families (e.g. sparse complete intersections / toric degenerations), compute their tropical limits and obstruction parameters, generate SAT encodings for solvability mod p^m, and check whether measured width/space lower bounds and solver hardness track the tropical parameter while families with the same classical algebraic invariants but different tropicalizations separate. Refutation occurs if no such correlation persists across controlled families or if width/space is better predicted by standard size/degree statistics alone. Impact: This would create a new bridge from tropical/algebraic geometry to proof complexity and SAT hardness, yielding geometry-aware hardness generators and potentially a new source of lower-bound techniques.",
