        "title": "Prime-Sensitive Spectral Drift in Families of Simplicial Complexes",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists an explicit infinite family of finite simplicial complexes K_n with integral boundary matrices such that the multiset of nonzero eigenvalues of the up-Laplacian over Q varies smoothly with n, but the reduction mod p exhibits sharp prime-dependent spectral jumps exactly at primes dividing torsion in H_*(K_n; Z). More precisely, for each n there is a computable set T_n of primes from torsion homology such that the mod-p Laplacian nullity and low-lying spectrum differ from the characteristic-0 pattern iff p in T_n. Test: Construct candidate families (e.g. arithmetic clique complexes, congruence quotient cell complexes, or mapping-torus complexes), compute integral homology and Laplacian spectra over Q and F_p for growing n, and check whether spectral discontinuities coincide exactly with torsion primes. Refutation occurs if spectral jumps persist at non-torsion primes or fail systematically at torsion primes. Impact: This would create a new bridge between combinatorial Hodge theory, torsion topology, and arithmetic detection, enabling spectral methods to predict hidden integral-topological structure and suggesting prime-aware algorithms for topological data analysis and quantum code design.",
