        "id": "fd_0368",
        "title": "Arithmetic Resonance Law for Torsion in Simplicial Configuration Spaces",
        "description": "Conjecture: For any finite connected graph G, let Conf_k(G) be the ordered configuration space of k distinct particles on G. There exists a graph-theoretic resonance invariant R_p(G,k), computable from cycle-intersection data modulo a prime p, such that p-torsion appears in H_i(Conf_k(G); Z) for some i if and only if R_p(G,k) exceeds an explicit threshold depending only on k and the local valence profile of G. Test: Compute H_i(Conf_k(G); Z) for broad graph families and k,p ranges, compare torsion support against the predicted invariant/threshold criterion, and search for counterexamples where torsion appears without resonance or resonance appears without torsion. Impact: This would create a new bridge between configuration-space topology, graph combinatorics, and arithmetic invariants, yielding a predictive theory for when discrete motion-planning spaces develop prime-specific topological obstructions.",
