        "id": "fd_0381",
        "title": "Noise Stability Threshold for Formal Proof Graphs",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let P be a corpus of formally verified proofs represented as dependency hypergraphs, and let T_\\varepsilon be the random perturbation that independently rewires each dependency edge with probability \\varepsilon while preserving degree sequence and type-correctness constraints. There exists a nontrivial critical threshold \\varepsilon_c \\in (0,1) such that for \\varepsilon < \\varepsilon_c, the distribution of minimal proof lengths for restating theorems in P changes by at most O(\\varepsilon), while for \\varepsilon > \\varepsilon_c, the median minimal proof length increases superlinearly in corpus size. Test: implement constrained rewiring on a large Lean/mathlib dependency graph, recompute shortest valid proof reconstructions or proxy search costs across a theorem benchmark, and estimate whether a sharp phase transition in reconstruction complexity occurs. Refutation is observed if complexity degrades smoothly without any threshold behavior across scales. Impact: would introduce a statistical-mechanical theory of proof-library robustness, guiding theorem-library architecture, retrieval design, and automated proof search under imperfect premises.",
