        "id": "fd_0391",
        "title": "Prime-Spectrum Rigidity of Integer Persistent Homology",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a universal constant C such that for any finite filtered simplicial complex K with total boundary-matrix bit-size N, the full integral persistent homology barcode in a fixed degree is uniquely determined by its reductions modulo all primes p <= C log N together with the rational barcode. Equivalently, no two non-isomorphic integer persistence modules of complexity N can agree over Q and over F_p for every prime p <= C log N. Test: Enumerate or construct pairs of filtered chain complexes with increasing N, compute persistence over Z, Q, and F_p for small primes, and search for the smallest counterexample; confirmation requires that reconstruction always succeeds from the truncated prime spectrum, while any pair with identical Q and small-prime data but different integral barcodes refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would give a sharply finite local-to-global principle for arithmetic topological data analysis, turning expensive integral persistence into a bounded set of field computations and revealing a new arithmetic rigidity phenomenon in filtered homological algebra.",
