        "id": "fd_0516",
        "title": "Renormalization Universality of Proof Search Across Formal Systems",
        "description": "Conjecture: For any two sufficiently expressive theorem-proving environments E1 and E2 (for example, Lean-style tactic search and first-order saturation), there exists a coarse-graining of proof states into finitely many mesoscopic features such that the normalized distribution of local search motifs (branching, backtracking loops, lemma-reuse bursts, dead-end depths) converges to the same universal scaling law on families of equivalent mathematical tasks translated between E1 and E2. Test: Build matched benchmark families by translating the same theorems into both environments, compute proof-state transition graphs, define several candidate coarse-grainings, and check whether motif count exponents and finite-size scaling collapses agree up to bounded error; a systematic failure of cross-system scaling agreement refutes the conjecture. Impact: Would suggest that proof search has system-independent statistical mechanics, enabling transferable search heuristics, principled benchmark design, and a new universality theory for automated reasoning.",
